        <div id="how-small" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Counts as a Small Yard
          </h2>

          <p>
            For mower shopping, a small yard is roughly anything under a quarter acre of actual grass, which is about 10,000 square feet. Many urban and suburban lots are much smaller once you subtract the house, driveway, patio and beds. A typical townhouse front yard might be 500 to 1,500 square feet; a small suburban backyard might be 2,000 to 5,000.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure the grass, not the lot. Pace out the lawn areas or use a satellite map tool to estimate square footage. That number tells you how much deck width, battery capacity or cord length you need. It also tells you whether a robot mower's area rating covers your lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Layout matters as much as size. A 3,000-square-foot lawn split into three small areas connected by narrow paths behaves differently from one open rectangle. Narrow side yards, gates, steps and terraces favor light, compact mowers that you can carry or steer easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your lawn is larger than a quarter acre, a full-size mower makes more sense. Our <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">self-propelled mower guide</a> covers lawns from a quarter to three quarters of an acre, and the <a href="/best-gas-lawn-mowers/">gas mower guide</a> covers larger lawns with thick grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth noting how often you will mow. A lawn mowed every five days in spring and every ten in midsummer has very different needs from one mowed whenever someone remembers. Frequent mowing favors light, simple machines such as reel and compact battery mowers. Infrequent mowing favors a mower with more power and a higher maximum cutting height, because it will be dealing with long grass.
          </p>

