        <div id="power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Choosing a Power Source
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery mowers are the default recommendation for most small yards. They start with a button, run quietly, need no fuel or oil and are often light enough to carry. A single battery usually handles a small lawn with regular mowing. If you already own tools from a battery platform, a mower on the same platform saves money.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded electric mowers are the cheapest powered option and have unlimited runtime while plugged in. They suit very small lawns near an outdoor outlet. The cord is the trade-off: you need a suitable extension cord and must keep it out of the mower's path.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reel mowers need no power at all. They cut cleanly and quietly and require little maintenance, but they struggle with tall grass, weeds and twigs and need frequent mowing. They suit small, smooth lawns of fine grasses mowed often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot mowers handle mowing automatically after setup and suit owners who would rather not mow at all. Gas mowers rarely make sense on small lawns because of their weight, noise, fuel storage and maintenance needs, unless the grass is very thick or the lawn often gets overgrown.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think about the rest of your yard tools too. If you already own a battery string trimmer or leaf blower, buying a mower from the same platform means one charger, interchangeable packs and a spare battery for the price of nothing. If you own nothing yet, choose a platform with the tools you are likely to want next, such as a trimmer for edges and a blower for paths and patios.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="battery-platforms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Battery Platforms and Runtime
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery mowers are sold by voltage and battery capacity. Voltage, typically 20, 40, 56, 60 or 80 volts, indicates the platform rather than a direct measure of cutting power, while capacity in amp-hours indicates how much energy the battery holds. Higher voltage platforms tend to support more powerful motors, and higher amp-hour batteries run longer.
          </p>

          <p>
            For small lawns, a 40-volt platform with a 4.0 amp-hour battery or similar is usually enough for a lawn mowed weekly. Larger or denser lawns benefit from bigger batteries or higher-voltage platforms. Manufacturers often list approximate coverage per charge; treat it as a best case, since tall or wet grass reduces runtime.
          </p>

          <p>
            Charging time matters if you need to finish in one session. Standard chargers can take an hour or more for a large battery, while rapid chargers cut that time. If your lawn is close to the limit of one battery, a second battery or a rapid charger avoids waiting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lithium-ion batteries gradually lose capacity with use and age. Storing them at moderate temperatures and partial charge helps them last. When a battery eventually needs replacing, the cost can be significant, which is another reason to consider a platform you will use for other tools as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushless motors, now common on mid-range battery mowers, run cooler and more efficiently than brushed motors. That usually means a little more runtime from the same battery and a longer motor life, which is worth a modest premium if you plan to keep the mower for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Deck Width for Small Lawns
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck width determines how much grass you cut per pass. Full-size mowers have 21- or 22-inch decks; compact mowers range from about 13 to 19 inches. On a small lawn, a narrower deck is often an advantage: it is lighter, turns more easily and fits through narrow spaces.
          </p>

          <p>
            For lawns under about 1,500 square feet, a 13- to 16-inch deck is plenty. The extra passes take only a few minutes, and the mower is easier to store and handle. For 1,500 to 5,000 square feet, a 16- to 19-inch deck balances coverage and maneuverability.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above about 5,000 square feet, a 19- to 21-inch deck saves time. Consider layout as well as area: a wide deck is efficient on open lawn but awkward on narrow strips and around many obstacles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the minimum and maximum cutting heights and how easy it is to adjust them. Single-lever height adjustment is convenient, and a higher maximum height helps in summer heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            A narrow deck also uses less battery for each foot of travel, so compact battery mowers often stretch a single charge further on a small lawn than their battery size suggests. That makes a 16-inch mower a sensible match for a modest battery, and it keeps the overall kit light enough to carry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-storage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Weight, Handling and Storage
          </h2>

          <p>
            In small yards, how a mower fits into your life often matters more than how it cuts. If the mower lives in a basement, a shed up a few steps or a small apartment storage space, weight is crucial. Compact battery and corded mowers can be light enough to carry easily; full-size self-propelled mowers are much heavier.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look for folding handles and vertical storage. Many battery mowers fold flat and can stand on end or hang on a wall, taking up very little floor space. Gas mowers generally cannot be stored on end unless specifically designed for it, because oil and fuel can leak.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider how you will move the mower to the lawn. Steps, gates and narrow paths favor lighter mowers with good handles. Larger rear wheels roll more easily over uneven ground and thresholds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery storage matters too. Store batteries indoors at a moderate temperature, especially over winter. Leaving lithium batteries in a freezing shed or hot garage shortens their life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before buying, measure the space where the mower will live, including any shelf or wall hook you plan to use. Check the folded dimensions in the product specifications rather than the operating dimensions, which include the extended handle. A mower that does not fit its storage space tends to end up outside, which shortens its life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="slopes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Slopes, Terraces and Rough Ground
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many small yards have slopes: terraced front lawns, steep side strips and banked backyards. Pushing a mower up these every week is tiring, and lighter mowers can slide on steep, wet grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sloped small yards, a self-propelled battery mower with rear-wheel drive, such as the EGO LM2135SP, is the strongest choice. Rear-wheel drive maintains traction when climbing. For very steep banks, a string trimmer may be safer and easier than any mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            With walk-behind mowers, mow across slopes rather than up and down, so the mower cannot roll back onto your feet if you slip. Avoid mowing wet slopes, and wear shoes with good grip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot mowers list a maximum slope in their specifications. Steep areas may need to be excluded with boundary wire. Reel mowers can be hard to push uphill and may not cut well on uneven ground.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small slopes are also where scalping happens most. A mower with a deck set too low catches the crest of a bump or the edge of a terrace and cuts the grass to the soil. Raising the cutting height a notch on uneven ground prevents bald patches and gives the lawn a more even look.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Are Reel Mowers Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Reel mowers appeal for good reasons: they are quiet, need no fuel or charging and give a clean, scissor-like cut. On small, smooth lawns of fine grasses mowed every few days, they can produce an excellent result.
          </p>

          <p>
            Their limitations are equally real. Reel mowers struggle with grass that has grown tall, with tough weeds and with twigs and debris, which can jam the reel. Coarse grasses such as some St. Augustine and Bahia lawns can be hard to cut cleanly.
          </p>

          <p>
            They require a consistent schedule. If you skip a week or go on vacation, you may need a powered mower or trimmer to catch up. Many reel mower owners keep a string trimmer for edges and occasional overgrowth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is simple but not zero. Reels and bed knives need periodic adjustment and occasional sharpening, and wheels and gears benefit from cleaning. If you enjoy mowing often and your lawn suits it, a reel mower is a satisfying, low-cost option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reel mowers also suit owners who want to avoid noise entirely. They can be used early in the morning or late in the evening without disturbing neighbors, and many owners enjoy the light exercise. If that matters to you and your lawn suits it, a reel mower is a pleasant tool rather than a compromise.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="corded" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Making a Corded Mower Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corded mowers offer unlimited runtime and low prices, but the cord needs managing. Use an outdoor-rated extension cord of the gauge and length the manual recommends. A cord that is too thin or too long can overheat or reduce power.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan a mowing pattern that works away from the outlet. Start nearest the outlet and mow in rows moving away, flipping the cord over the mower handle as you turn so it stays on the cut side. A cord retainer on the handle helps prevent the plug pulling out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug into a GFCI-protected outlet, as electrical codes require for outdoor receptacles, and inspect the cord for cuts or damage before each use. Never mow over the cord, and do not mow wet grass with a corded mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Corded mowers suit small, simple lawns close to an outlet. If your lawn has many obstacles or is far from an outlet, the cord becomes a nuisance, and a battery mower is the better choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the extension cord coiled on a reel or hook, out of sunlight, so it does not crack or tangle. A cord in good condition is safer and easier to manage, and a bright color makes it more visible on the lawn, reducing the chance of running over it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="robots" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Robot Mowers for Small Yards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Robot mowers mow small amounts often, on a schedule, and return to a base to charge. The result is a consistently tidy lawn with fine clippings that disappear. They are quiet enough to run while you are home.
          </p>

          <p>
            Setup is the main commitment. Most models in the mainstream price range use a boundary wire pegged or buried around the lawn edge and around beds and obstacles. Some newer models use GPS or vision systems instead, but they often cost more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the area rating against your lawn, and check the maximum slope. Narrow passages, complex layouts and multiple separate lawn areas can require extra setup or may not work well.
          </p>

          <p>
            You will still need a trimmer for edges and around obstacles. Keep children and pets supervised when the robot is running, and follow the manufacturer's safety guidance. If you want to stop mowing entirely and are willing to invest the setup time, a robot can be a good fit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Theft and weather are worth thinking about too. Many robot mowers include PIN codes and alarms, and some have GPS tracking. Rain sensors on some models send the mower home during wet weather, which protects the lawn and the machine. Check which features the model you are considering includes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="noise-neighbors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Noise, Neighbors and Shared Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Small yards often mean close neighbors. A gas mower running under a bedroom window at eight on a Saturday morning is a common source of friction. Battery, corded and reel mowers are much quieter, which makes it easier to mow at times that suit you without bothering anyone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some homeowners associations and municipalities set quiet hours or noise limits for outdoor power equipment. Check your local rules, especially in townhouse and condominium communities where landscaping rules can be specific.
          </p>

          <p>
            Shared spaces bring their own considerations. If you share a lawn or a storage area, a light mower that anyone can lift and start with a button is more practical than a gas mower that needs fuel and a strong pull. Consider who else will use the mower when choosing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot mowers raise a different consideration: they run unattended, sometimes when neighbors and their pets or children are nearby. Schedule them for times when the yard is quiet, and make sure the boundary keeps the mower within your property.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, clippings and debris on shared paths and sidewalks are a common complaint. Blow or sweep them back onto the lawn after mowing, and direct the discharge away from walkways, parked cars and neighbors' windows while you work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mowing-well" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. Mowing a Small Lawn Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            Cut no more than about a third of the grass height at a time. Removing more stresses the grass and leaves clumps of clippings. In spring, when growth is fast, that may mean mowing more than once a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mow with a sharp blade. A dull blade tears grass, leaving ragged tips that turn brown. Sharpen or replace blades at least once a season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wait for dry grass before you mow. Damp clippings stick together, pack into the deck and make footing slippery. It also drains battery mowers faster.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vary your mowing pattern to avoid ruts and to help grass grow upright. Leave clippings on the lawn when possible; they return nutrients and reduce the need for fertilizer. Raise the cutting height in summer heat to help the lawn tolerate drought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Edges make a small lawn look finished. A string trimmer or a pair of edging shears tidies the borders along paths and beds that a mower cannot reach. Because small lawns have a high ratio of edge to area, good edging often makes a bigger visual difference than the mowing itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upkeep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Upkeep for Small-Yard Mowers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Battery and corded mowers need little maintenance. Keep the deck clean, sharpen or replace the blade, check that bolts are tight and store the mower dry. Clean vents on the motor housing so it does not overheat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Batteries last longest when stored at moderate temperatures and partial charge over winter. Avoid leaving them on the charger indefinitely unless the manufacturer says it is fine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reel mowers need the reel-to-bed-knife contact adjusted periodically and occasional sharpening, which can be done with a backlapping kit or by a shop. Keep the reel clean and the wheels free of debris.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot mowers need blade changes, cleaning of the underside and wheels, and occasional checks of the boundary wire. Many owners bring them indoors over winter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever the mower, check the blade bolt and handle fasteners at the start of each season. Vibration loosens hardware over time, and a loose blade is both a safety risk and a cause of poor cut quality.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-owner" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Which Type Suits Which Owner
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best small-yard mower depends as much on the owner as the lawn. Someone who mows every Saturday morning without fail, has a smooth lawn of fine grass and enjoys a quiet task is a natural reel mower owner. Someone who travels often and lets the lawn go for two weeks at a time needs a powered mower with enough muscle to recover from long grass.
          </p>

          <p>
            Renters and apartment-with-a-yard owners usually do best with a compact battery or corded mower. Both are light, need no fuel storage, and move easily between homes. A robot mower is rarely worth installing in a rental, since the boundary wire stays behind or has to be pulled up when you leave.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owners with limited strength or mobility should prioritize light weight, push-button starting and an easy height adjuster. A light battery mower or a self-propelled battery model removes the hardest parts of mowing. A robot can take mowing off the list entirely, though setup may need help.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget-first buyers with a tiny lawn near an outlet will find a corded mower hard to beat. Everyone else with a small lawn will usually be happiest with a compact battery mower, and should spend any extra money on a second battery or a matching trimmer rather than a bigger deck.
          </p>

