        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            15. What to Spend
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corded mowers are the cheapest powered option, often roughly $100 to $250 at the time of writing. Reel mowers typically fall in a similar range, with premium models costing more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compact battery mowers with a battery and charger typically cost about $200 to $400. Full-size battery self-propelled mowers cost more, often $500 to $800 or above.
          </p>

          <p>
            Robot mowers start at several hundred dollars and can exceed $1,000, with installation costs if you pay for setup. Factor in the time and effort of boundary wire installation if you do it yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider long-term costs: replacement batteries for cordless mowers, blades, extension cords and, for robots, replacement blades and possibly wire repairs. For most small yards, a compact battery mower offers the best balance of cost and convenience.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buying at the end of the season can save money. Many retailers discount mowers in late summer and fall to clear space, and battery kits often go on sale around major holidays. If your current mower still works, waiting for a sale is a low-risk way to get a better model for the same budget.
          </p>

