        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a mower sized for a big lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy 21-inch self-propelled gas mower is overkill for a small lawn. It is harder to store, carry and maneuver. Measure your grass area and choose a compact battery, corded or reel mower unless your lawn has slopes or thick grass. A lighter machine is easier to carry and store.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Picking a reel mower for an irregular schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Reel mowers work best on grass mowed every few days. If you often skip weeks, tall grass will jam the reel and leave an uneven cut. Choose a powered mower if your schedule is inconsistent. Tall grass simply jams the reel. A missed week can mean a second mower is needed to catch up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating robot mower setup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most robot mowers need a boundary wire installed around the lawn and obstacles. Narrow passages, slopes and multiple lawn areas add complexity. Plan for an afternoon of setup and check the slope and area ratings first. Factor in time to trim edges the robot cannot reach as well.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong extension cord</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Corded mowers need an outdoor-rated extension cord of the right gauge and length. A cord that is too thin or too long can overheat or reduce power. Plug into a GFCI-protected outlet and inspect the cord before each use. Check the manual for the recommended gauge and length.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing batteries in extreme temperatures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lithium batteries lose capacity faster when stored in freezing or very hot conditions. Store batteries indoors at moderate temperatures, especially over winter, to extend their life. A cool basement or utility room shelf is ideal. Avoid leaving packs on a charger in a hot garage all summer, too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which mower suits most small lawns?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most small lawns mowed regularly, a compact battery mower like the Greenworks 40V 16-inch is the most practical choice: light, quiet, easy to store and maintenance-free. If your small yard has slopes or thick grass, the EGO LM2135SP's rear-wheel drive and stronger motor are worth the extra cost. Either will fold for compact storage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What cutting width works for a small lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For lawns under about 1,500 square feet, a 13- to 16-inch deck is plenty. For 1,500 to 5,000 square feet, 16 to 19 inches balances coverage and maneuverability. Above about 5,000 square feet, a 19- to 21-inch deck saves time. Narrow strips and many obstacles favor narrower decks. Measure before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a reel mower make sense for my yard?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A reel mower makes sense for a small, smooth lawn of fine grasses that you mow every few days. It is quiet, needs no fuel or charging and gives a clean cut. It struggles with tall grass, weeds and twigs and is a poor fit if your schedule is irregular. Keep a trimmer for catching up after a missed week.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I get a robot mower for a small lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A robot mower can be a good fit if you want to stop mowing and are willing to install a boundary wire. Check the area and slope ratings against your lawn, and expect to still use a trimmer for edges. For lawns with complex layouts, setup can be more involved. Many owners hire installation help for the first setup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does gas ever make sense on a compact lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely. Gas mowers are heavier, louder and need fuel and oil maintenance. They make sense on small lawns only if the grass is very thick, often overgrown or the yard is large enough to strain battery runtime. For most small yards, battery or corded mowers are easier to own. Fuel storage is another hassle in small homes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a small lawn be mowed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mow often enough that you remove no more than about a third of the grass height at a time. During peak growth in spring, that may mean every five to seven days or more often. In summer heat or drought, growth slows and you can mow less frequently, with a higher cutting height.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a battery last on a small lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A single battery from a reputable brand usually handles a small lawn with regular mowing. Runtime depends on battery capacity, grass height and moisture. If your lawn is near the upper end of small or the grass is often long, consider a larger battery or a spare. Rapid chargers also help on bigger lawns.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

